    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Trevor Story** (70.40) and **Corbin Carroll** (68.74) headlining our offensive attack. **Trevor Story** finds himself in a prime position against Nick Martinez; with a 4.56 xERA opposing him and a park factor boost, he is our clear efficiency leader. Meanwhile, **Corbin Carroll** is a mandatory start despite facing a disciplined defensive catcher. While his score is suppressed slightly by the 1.87s pop-time risk, his elite sprint speed and favorable platoon matchup against Brazob\u00e1n remain the engine of our stolen base pursuit. **James Wood** also demands attention at 68.42; hitting leadoff against Alcantara provides the highest volume of plate appearances, and his strong .377 xwOBA combined with a favorable 4.45 xERA matchup makes him a core component of our daily floor.\n\nRegarding our bench decisions, **Travis Bazzana** (44.91) was the most difficult exclusion from the active roster. While his xwOBA of .382 suggests he is primed for a breakout, he narrowly lost out to **Alex Freeland** and **Dalton Rushing** based on the specific platoon and order-slot requirements calculated for today\u2019s matchups. **Mickey Moniak** fell further down the board, finishing below our 40.0 floor due to a brutal combination of a tough matchup against Cristopher S\u00e1nchez and significant platoon disadvantage, forcing us to preserve his game cap for a more advantageous scenario.\n\nWe are keeping a close watch on **Seiya Suzuki** (44.97), as the matchup against deGrom is a high-variance event. The elite skill of the opposing pitcher has dragged **Seiya Suzuki**\u2019s score near our bench threshold, and his output is heavily contingent on his ability to navigate an ace-level start. Similarly, **Jose Caballero** (41.62) is skating on thin ice today; while his speed is always a threat, Henderson\u2019s 2.87 xERA represents the toughest test on the board for our starters. We expect high-leverage production from **Vinnie Pasquantino** (61.44) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (63.88), both of whom benefit from positive platoon dynamics and favorable park settings to stabilize our daily output.",
